AA: a non-aliasing atomic store (release or weaker) does not clobber a location

AAResults::getModRefInfo(StoreInst) returned ModRef for every location as
soon as the store's ordering was stronger than unordered, before consulting
alias information at all. But ModRef describes data effects on the queried
location: a store that provably does not alias Loc cannot modify it, and an
ordering of release or weaker constrains only program-order-earlier accesses,
imposing nothing on unrelated memory. Consult the alias result first and
return NoModRef for a proven-NoAlias location unless the store is seq_cst
(which also participates in the global total order of seq_cst operations).

This lets e.g. LICM hoist an invariant load out of a loop whose body performs
a release store to unrelated memory (the publication-store idiom); moving a
program-order-later read above a release store is permitted by the memory
model. Note the asymmetry: passes that would *sink* a program-order-earlier
access past a release store must (and do) justify that movement by the moved
instruction's own ordering, not by this query.

Experimental patch carried on Julia's kf/retyped-globals-flagcheck branch to
measure the cost of runtime re-type guards (JuliaLang/julia#62154).

diff --git a/lib/Analysis/AliasAnalysis.cpp b/lib/Analysis/AliasAnalysis.cpp
--- a/lib/Analysis/AliasAnalysis.cpp
+++ b/lib/Analysis/AliasAnalysis.cpp
@@ -438,16 +438,23 @@
 ModRefInfo AAResults::getModRefInfo(const StoreInst *S,
                                     const MemoryLocation &Loc,
                                     AAQueryInfo &AAQI) {
-  // Be conservative in the face of atomic.
-  if (isStrongerThan(S->getOrdering(), AtomicOrdering::Unordered))
-    return ModRefInfo::ModRef;
-
   if (Loc.Ptr) {
     AliasResult AR = alias(MemoryLocation::get(S), Loc, AAQI, S);
     // If the store address cannot alias the pointer in question, then the
-    // specified memory cannot be modified by the store.
+    // specified memory cannot be modified by the store. This holds for atomic
+    // stores as well: an ordering of release or weaker constrains only
+    // program-order-earlier accesses and says nothing about the contents of
+    // unrelated memory, so it must not turn a non-aliasing store into a
+    // clobber of Loc. Remain conservative for seq_cst stores, which also
+    // participate in the global total order of seq_cst operations.
     if (AR == AliasResult::NoAlias)
-      return ModRefInfo::NoModRef;
+      return isStrongerThan(S->getOrdering(), AtomicOrdering::Release)
+                 ? ModRefInfo::ModRef
+                 : ModRefInfo::NoModRef;
+
+    // Be conservative in the face of atomic.
+    if (isStrongerThan(S->getOrdering(), AtomicOrdering::Unordered))
+      return ModRefInfo::ModRef;
 
     // Examine the ModRef mask. If Mod isn't present, then return NoModRef.
     // This ensures that if Loc is a constant memory location, we take into
@@ -455,8 +462,15 @@
     // location.
     if (!isModSet(getModRefInfoMask(Loc)))
       return ModRefInfo::NoModRef;
+
+    // Otherwise, a store just writes.
+    return ModRefInfo::Mod;
   }
 
+  // Be conservative in the face of atomic.
+  if (isStrongerThan(S->getOrdering(), AtomicOrdering::Unordered))
+    return ModRefInfo::ModRef;
+
   // Otherwise, a store just writes.
   return ModRefInfo::Mod;
 }
